Found while doing #100, which gave `karr materialize` the tracked-path check init got under #89, via a new `BoardStore::project_owned_view_paths`.

The check walks the file-view directory and asks git one `is_tracked` per file. Measured on a 300-card board, repeat materialize: 1190ms -> 1370ms, ~0.6ms per card, linear, short-circuiting on the first tracked file.

Deliberately not optimised in #100, and that judgement still holds: materialize is an explicit bridge command, not a hot loop. Note the obvious cheap guard -- only ask when an entry would actually be added -- does NOT help, because in the bug scenario the entries never get added, so the walk runs on every invocation regardless.

The real fix is to ask the index instead of the working tree: one read for any tracked path under the file-view directory, instead of N status calls. It would also close a documented gap in the current check -- a path git tracks but that is missing from the working tree is not found by a directory walk, because there is no file to walk onto.

Cost: new App::karr::Git API, and it changes `karr init`s behaviour too (both commands share the helper), which #100 explicitly ruled out of its own scope. Hence a separate ticket.

Not urgent. File it, do it when Git grows index access for another reason.
Landed on main as 2b29403 (combined with the 0.500 version bump and the other tickets in that commit). Full suite green: 113 files, 767 tests.
